module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.FaithfullyFlat.Algebra
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Quotient
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Flatness.EtaleLocalization.FlatHenselization
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.FlatModuleBaseChangeAlgebra

/-!
# Flatness of a fibre of a module under a base change over a pushout of rings

Let `R → A` and `R → R'` be ring homomorphisms, let `T` be the pushout `A ⊗_R R'`, let `A'` be a
flat `T`-algebra, and let `I` be an ideal of `R` which becomes zero in `R'`, so that `R'` and `A'`
are algebras over the quotients `R/I` and `A/IA`.  For an `A`-module `M` the two conditions

* `M/IA·M` is flat over `R/I`,
* `A' ⊗_A M` is flat over `R'`

are then equivalent, provided `R'` is flat over `R/I` and `A/IA → A'` is a local homomorphism of
local rings.

This is the commutative algebra behind the comparison of conditions (1) and (2) of Stacks 05WY.
There `R`, `A` are the étale local rings `𝒪_{Y, ȳ}`, `𝒪_{X, x̄}` of a morphism `f : X ⟶ Y` of
algebraic spaces at a geometric point, `I` is the ideal `𝔪_{z̄}𝒪_{Y, ȳ}` generated by the maximal
ideal of the étale local ring of a third space `Z` under `h : Y ⟶ Z`, `R'` and `A'` are the étale
local rings of the fibres `Y_z = Spec(K) ×_Z Y` and `X_z = Spec(K) ×_Z X` over a field-valued
point `z` of `Z`, and `M` is the geometric stalk `F_{x̄}`.  The first condition is then
condition (1) of Stacks 05WY, which this development spells
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsFibreFlatOverAtGeometricPoint`, and the second is condition (2) read
at a geometric point through Stacks 05VU and the base-change formula of Stacks 05VQ.

## Main results

* `Algebra.IsPushout.quotIdealMap`: `A/IA` is the pushout of `A` along `R → R/I`.
* `Algebra.IsPushout.cancel`: a pushout square cancels out of a composite pushout square.
* `TensorProduct.quotSMulTensorEquiv`: `A' ⊗_{A/J} (M/JM) = A' ⊗_A M` when `J` acts as zero on
  `A'`.
* `Module.Flat.quotient_of_isPushout`: flatness over `R/I` of the pushout `T`, from flatness of
  `R'` over `R/I`.
* `Module.Flat.quotSMul_iff_tensor_of_isPushout`: the equivalence of the two conditions.

## Implementation notes

The two implications are the fibre analogues of the two parts of Stacks 05VW, and are obtained
from the same two inputs as those, applied over the quotients rather than over `R` and `A`:

* `M/IA·M` flat over `R/I` gives `A' ⊗_{A/IA} (M/IA·M)` flat over `R'` because `A'` is flat over
  the pushout `(A/IA) ⊗_{R/I} R'`, which is `Module.Flat.tensorProduct_of_isPushout`;
* conversely flatness over `R'` gives flatness over `R/I` because `R'` is flat over `R/I`, and
  then descends through the faithfully flat `A/IA → A'` by Stacks 0584 (2),
  `Module.Flat.tensorProduct_iff_of_faithfullyFlat`.

That the pushout `A ⊗_R R'` is *also* the pushout `(A/IA) ⊗_{R/I} R'` is the only step that is
not already available: it is the cancellation `Algebra.IsPushout.cancel` of the pushout square
exhibiting `A/IA` as `(R/I) ⊗_R A`, which is `Algebra.IsPushout.quotIdealMap`.  Correspondingly
`A/IA → A'` is flat because `A'` is flat over the pushout and the pushout is flat over `A/IA` by
base change of the flatness of `R'` over `R/I`; being a local homomorphism of local rings it is
then faithfully flat, which is where the hypotheses `IsLocalRing` and `IsLocalHom` enter.

Flatness of `R'` over `R/I` is a hypothesis here.  In the intended application it comes from the
same cancellation: `R'` is flat over `𝒪_{Y, ȳ} ⊗_{𝒪_{Z, z̄}} 𝒪_{Spec(K), k̄}`, the last factor is
a field in which `𝔪_{z̄}` is zero, and that tensor product is therefore the base change of `R/I`
along the extension of the residue field of `𝒪_{Z, z̄}` by that field, hence flat over `R/I`;
`Module.Flat.quotient_of_isPushout` is that step.

The module structure of `R/I` on `M/IA·M` is restriction of scalars along the induced
homomorphism `R/I → A/IA`, which is the one `Module.FlatQuotSMul` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/FibreFlatnessGeometricPoint.lean` uses; it is supplied by
the statement so that no instance has to be in scope at the call site.

